Discussion

The team reviewed the Hamming (7,4) implementation and discussed how the assembly program works with the existing simulator.

The following implementation details were reviewed:

* Four data bits (D1, D2, D3, D4) are used as input.
* Three parity bits (P1, P2, P4) are generated using XOR operations.
* An intentional error is introduced to test error detection.
* Syndrome bits (S1, S2, S4) are calculated to identify the error position.
* The Java ALERT instruction displays the detection result in the UI.

The team also reviewed two demonstration programs:

* Hamming74Demo.asm: Tests error detection at bit position 3.
* Hamming74NoError.asm: Tests the no-error condition.

The expected outputs were discussed:

* Error scenario: Syndrome 011 – Error detected at bit position 3.
* No-error scenario: Syndrome 000 – No error detected.

Decisions

1. The assembly programs will use supported simulator instructions such as MOV, XRL, ALERT, and END.
2. The Hamming calculations will remain in assembly rather than using special Hamming pseudo-instructions.
3. Java will handle the required instruction execution and UI alert display.
4. The existing Week 1–3 and Week 4 project structure must be preserved.
5. The team will verify both test scenarios before finalizing the implementation.

